WEBVTT

00:06.920 --> 00:10.560
A classical bit takes one of two values: zero or one.

00:11.080 --> 00:12.640
A qubit has the same two states.

00:13.360 --> 00:17.000
They are written in bra-ket notation, and together they are called the computational basis.

00:19.960 --> 00:23.780
Before it is measured, however, a qubit can hold both values at the same time.

00:24.060 --> 00:28.240
Not as two separate things: as one state that carries a weight on each of the two outcomes.

00:30.960 --> 00:33.160
Those two weights constitute the entire state.

00:33.500 --> 00:36.820
When both are nonzero, the qubit is in a superposition state.

00:43.900 --> 00:45.940
But, what is physically a qubit?

00:48.120 --> 00:52.220
A qubit may be realised in many physical systems with two distinguishable states.

00:54.460 --> 00:57.060
The ground and excited states of a cold atom.

00:57.440 --> 01:01.260
The two spin orientations of a single electron confined in a quantum dot.

01:01.700 --> 01:04.340
The horizontal and vertical polarisation of a photon.

01:06.720 --> 01:10.520
All three are described by the same two basis states and the same weighted combination.

01:11.160 --> 01:14.600
The remainder of this film therefore concerns the theoretical description of a qubit,

01:14.620 --> 01:15.800
and not its hardware.

01:22.860 --> 01:25.140
Alpha and beta are complex numbers.

01:25.660 --> 01:28.900
Each therefore carries two quantities: a magnitude and a phase.

01:29.700 --> 01:32.920
These two complex numbers are the probability amplitudes of the qubit.

01:35.400 --> 01:37.240
The magnitudes are not independent.

01:42.620 --> 01:45.260
Squared, they sum to one, without exception.

01:50.680 --> 01:52.100
A second constraint applies.

01:52.560 --> 01:56.920
Multiplying the entire state by a common phase factor changes nothing that can be measured.

02:02.480 --> 02:04.900
That phase is therefore not part of the state.

02:14.240 --> 02:15.260
Consider the count.

02:15.780 --> 02:18.200
Two complex numbers amount to four real parameters.

02:21.860 --> 02:23.480
Normalisation of the magnitudes removes one.

02:23.500 --> 02:26.360
The irrelevance of the overall phase removes a second.

02:28.880 --> 02:29.680
Two remain.

02:30.280 --> 02:33.500
And two real parameters are precisely what specify a point on a sphere.

02:42.860 --> 02:45.020
This is that sphere: the Bloch sphere.

02:45.540 --> 02:47.840
The state is an arrow from the centre to the surface,

02:48.360 --> 02:50.300
zero at the north pole and one at the south.

02:50.620 --> 02:54.080
The first angle, theta, is measured downward from the north pole,

02:54.560 --> 02:55.860
and it fixes the two magnitudes.

02:58.480 --> 03:02.480
At the north pole the outcome is certainly zero; at the south pole, certainly one;

03:02.660 --> 03:05.040
on the equator, the two outcomes are equally likely.

03:10.500 --> 03:13.280
The second angle, phi, runs around the equator.

03:13.660 --> 03:15.860
It is the phase difference between the two amplitudes.

03:16.420 --> 03:19.560
Theta ranges from zero to pi, and phi from zero to two pi.

03:20.520 --> 03:22.440
Together they cover the sphere exactly once.

03:23.180 --> 03:24.840
The length of the arrow never changes.

03:25.400 --> 03:26.580
Only its direction does.

03:33.700 --> 03:35.000
Now consider a measurement.

03:35.380 --> 03:38.600
The result is either zero or one, and never an intermediate value.

03:39.040 --> 03:40.660
The measurement also acts on the qubit.

03:41.280 --> 03:45.100
Immediately afterwards it is in the state that was returned, and the superposition is gone.

03:47.240 --> 03:51.180
The probability of each outcome is the square of the corresponding magnitude.

03:56.560 --> 04:00.520
Prepare many identical qubits in the same state and measure them one at a time.

04:00.860 --> 04:04.880
As their number grows, the observed proportions converge on the predicted probabilities.

04:14.300 --> 04:16.860
Suppose that the same qubit is measured a second time.

04:17.200 --> 04:18.880
The result is the same as the first,

04:19.240 --> 04:22.300
and it remains the same however often the measurement is repeated.

04:24.960 --> 04:29.380
The superposition is gone, and unless the qubit is prepared again in a superposition,

04:29.760 --> 04:33.860
its state is now the outcome itself, and every further measurement simply returns it.

04:40.920 --> 04:41.820
To sum up.

04:42.320 --> 04:46.740
The state of a qubit is two complex amplitudes, one for each of the two outcomes.

04:50.540 --> 04:53.580
Normalisation and the irrelevance of a common phase leave two real parameters:

04:54.080 --> 04:57.160
the angles theta and phi, which place a point on the Bloch sphere.

04:59.800 --> 05:04.500
A measurement returns zero or one, with probabilities given by the squared magnitudes,

05:05.060 --> 05:06.960
and leaves the qubit in the state it returned.
